WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.756
Step one of clustering: which pairs belong together?

2
00:00:07.756 --> 00:00:12.356
Give every pair a score, then rank them from high to low.

3
00:00:12.356 --> 00:00:16.489
Note: a score ranks pairs; it does not group them.

4
00:00:16.889 --> 00:00:22.356
One: direct weight. The score is the weight of the wire.

5
00:00:22.356 --> 00:00:26.044
G01's six edges give six pairs.

6
00:00:26.044 --> 00:00:33.289
A–B and D–E tie at 5: sorted by (−score, u, v), A–B comes first.

7
00:00:33.689 --> 00:00:36.489
Two: shared neighbours.

8
00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:39.289
A–C: the direct wire is 1.

9
00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:45.867
Add the link through B, at its weaker wire: min(5, 4) = 4.

10
00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:47.667
Total: 5.

11
00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:50.867
B and D share no wire,

12
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:55.444
but through C they get min(4, 2) = 2.

13
00:00:55.444 --> 00:01:01.133
So check all 10 pairs: a pair with no wire can still score.

14
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:08.333
Change the score and the first pair changes. Look at the original author's priority graph.

15
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:12.244
Direct weight: X–Y = 5 comes first.

16
00:01:12.244 --> 00:01:17.933
Shared neighbours: P–Q = 4 + 4 = 8 overtakes X–Y.

17
00:01:18.333 --> 00:01:26.022
Three, for multi-pin nets: two wide 5-pin nets and one private net.

18
00:01:26.022 --> 00:01:29.933
Share each net out over |e| − 1.

19
00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:35.844
A–B gets only 1/4 + 1/4 = 0.5; E–F gets 1.

20
00:01:36.244 --> 00:01:38.378
Four things to keep.

21
00:01:38.378 --> 00:01:41.044
It ranks; it does not group;

22
00:01:41.044 --> 00:01:43.644
the ranking must be stable;

23
00:01:43.644 --> 00:01:51.511
a different score can pick a different first pair, and a score is not a distance. Try it in the lab below.
